A||||6||Adrift||$650 on suspension upgrades. What to buy?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-23-2001||01:59 PM||||I have all the wheels and brakes off, so I would like to upgrade the suspension on a 355-style kit car which I'm building. For $650, What should I buy? Please include web addresses as well.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Ari<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick's Dad||12-23-2001||02:31 PM||||Depends on what you want to do, and what year your car is. Take a look at Held Motorsports <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.heldmotorsports.com</A>  for high end suspension stuff, much of it priced too high, but you migh be able to squeeze in lowering spindles and rear coil-over conversions.<P>Also, depending on your model, simply leaving the suspension stock and getting stiffer springs with anti-sway bars (or larger ones), better shocks and/or poly bushings may be the thing.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  has springs and shocks, or, with a little research, you can probably find them slightly cheaper.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.energysuspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.energysuspension.com</A>  doesn't list a lot of the polygraphite bushings, but there must be a place for them... someone else?||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||12-23-2001||03:54 PM||||Ari: 650 CDN doesn't go very far. Best to replace any worn out stuff you can. Poly bushings also at <A HREF="http://www.8shark.com" TARGET=_blank>www.8shark.com</A>  and you can get aluminum cradle bushings from Darrell Morse. You could mount a rear anti sway bar as well.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-23-2001||05:22 PM||||before you ask what you can do.... what have you already got?<P>I'd strongly recomend new shocks and struts at the least. I went with Gabriel on mine. Others like Monroe or KYB.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-24-2001||03:58 AM||||What year?  If you're building a 355 replica, with the associated track increases, you really need to be talking to Dave Held and getting his widened suspension components.<P>It is THE ONLY way to do it properly, but it's more expensive.  Worth every penny though, and still a good value for what you get!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Adrift||12-24-2001||11:49 AM||||its a 86 with everything stock (and rusting away!)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||12-24-2001||01:32 PM||||Unless you're changing the track for the kit car, a good budget upgrade would be lowering springs, KYB shocks/struts and poly front and rear.  That'll make a HUGH difference in handling.  You may also need Moog Problem Solver front ball joints to get your alignment correct with the lowering springs, but I've ridden in an 87 GT with these mods, and they really work well, especially for the money.||||reg||3
